Wrote `labels/coder-1.json` — one row, Josh Becker / `ai-regulation`, coded **BLANK `direction-only`**.

The single source is the chaptered text of SB 942 (2023-2024), the California AI Transparency Act, whose Legislative Counsel's Digest carries the author line `SB 942, Becker. California AI Transparency Act.` — so V1 `own-act`, V3 `record` / `record_kind: author`, V5 `in-term` (chaptered 2024-09-19, within his term from 2020-12-07). No tally quote: the page prints no vote, and an author group needs none.

The reason it can't seat a chair: SB 942's operative duties are an AI detection tool plus manifest and latent provenance disclosures, enforced by a per-violation civil penalty. A binding, penalty-backed mandate excludes the "freely without government interference" and "let companies choose whether to follow them" rungs, but a disclosure duty is not liability "when their systems cause harm" (the penalty attaches to non-disclosure), not pre-deployment "safety testing… in high-stakes areas", and not "strict government approval… before any AI system can be deployed". That separates the sides without separating the three rungs on the oversight side — V4 `direction-only`.

`needs_source` asks for his own answer to the ladder question and for roll calls or authorship on AI bills whose operative sections are safety testing or developer liability. No quotes: the only snapshot is bill text, not his words.
